concepts.content:
Self-attention is an architecture where all tokens in a layer submit queries, and for each query, a weighted sum is taken over all tokens in that layer. If $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}$ contains $N$ tokens, the layer produces $N$ queries, $N$ weighted sums, and $N$ output tokens. (see figure: A self-attention layer.)

To compute the query, key, and value matrices, we apply linear transformations to the input tokens:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_q^\mathsf{T} \\
    \mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_k^\mathsf{T} \\
    \mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_v^\mathsf{T}
\end{aligned}$$

The attention matrix $\mathbf{A}$ and output $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}$ are calculated as:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{A} &= \texttt{softmax}\Big(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}}^\mathsf{T}}{\sqrt{m}}\Big) \\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}} &= \mathbf{A}\mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}}
\end{aligned}$$

Intuitively, self-attention allows tokens to refine their representations by attending to relevant context from other tokens. For example, in image segmentation, a token representing an animal's torso can attend to other patches of the same animal to reinforce its classification. (see figure: Self-attention layer expanded.) (see figure: One way self-attention could be used to aggregate information...) (see figure: Example of self-attention maps...)

